## What usually comes next?

The simplest way to predict the next word is to look at the word you just read, and remember what
came after it in the past. If *little* was followed by *lamb* once, by *dog* once, and by *star*
once, then after *little* each of those has a one in three chance.

That's a <GlossaryPopover term="bigram">bigram model</GlossaryPopover>: *bi* because it only ever
looks at two words, the current one and the next.<Cite id="jurafsky-martin-ngrams" /> Building one
takes nothing but counting. Below, the training text is six nursery rhymes. Step through it (or
press *Play*) to watch the counts fill in, one pair of words at a time.

<BigramBuilder />

## From counts to probabilities

<At levels="simple">

Once everything is counted, each word's counts turn into chances: after *little*, the three
followers each get one in three. Pick a word in the widget to see its chances as bars. To make up
text, start with a word, pick the next one using those chances, and repeat.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Once everything is counted, each row turns into probabilities: divide each count by the row's total.
That's all the model knows. Pick a word in the widget to see its row as bars.

To generate text, start with a word, spin for the next one using those probabilities, then repeat
with the new word. Claude Shannon generated English this way in 1948, choosing each word based only
on the one before it.<Cite id="shannon1948" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### The counting rule

As a formula, the bigram model's chance for the next word is

$$
P(w_i \mid w_{i-1}) = \frac{C(w_{i-1}\,w_i)}{C(w_{i-1})}
$$

where $w_{i-1}$ is the word just read, $w_i$ is a candidate next word, $C(w_{i-1}\,w_i)$ is how many
times that pair appears in the training text, and $C(w_{i-1})$ is how many times $w_{i-1}$ appears
with any word after it. For *little*, $C(\text{little lamb}) = 1$ and $C(\text{little}) = 3$, so
$P(\text{lamb} \mid \text{little}) = 1/3$. These are the chances that make the training text as
likely as possible.<Cite id="jurafsky-martin-ngrams" />

Real n-gram models look further back (a trigram uses the last two words) and deal with pairs they
never saw. Here, any unseen pair gets a chance of 0, and since a sentence's chance is its words'
chances multiplied, one new pair makes the whole sentence impossible. Smoothing moves a little
probability onto unseen pairs. The simplest version adds one to every count:

$$
P_{\text{add-1}}(w_i \mid w_{i-1}) = \frac{C(w_{i-1}\,w_i) + 1}{C(w_{i-1}) + V}
$$

where $V$ is the number of different words.<Cite id="jurafsky-martin-ngrams" />

Counting also stops scaling. With GPT-2's 50,257 tokens, a table of every pair would have about
2.5 billion cells, and a table of every triple about 127 trillion. Bengio and colleagues called this
the curse of dimensionality, and proposed learning to compute a prediction instead of looking one
up.<Cite id="bengio2003" />

</At>

## Why the output is bad

Press *Generate* a few times. The output borrows phrases from the rhymes, but it wanders between
them: *the cow jumped over the clock struck one, the lamb was sure to go*. Every pair of words
appeared in the training text, but the sentence as a whole makes no sense.

The problem is that the model only ever looks one word back. After *the*, it has no idea whether it
is in the rhyme about the cow, the mouse, or the lamb, so it picks a follower of *the* from all of
them.

<RecapCard>

- Prediction means answering "what usually comes next?"
- A bigram model answers it by counting which word follows which, then turning the counts into
  probabilities.
- It only looks one word back, so its output is locally plausible but wanders.

<At levels="deep">

- $P(w_i \mid w_{i-1}) = C(w_{i-1}\,w_i) / C(w_{i-1})$: the pair's count over the first word's
  count.

</At>
</RecapCard>

